  "subtitle": "A standalone wave-computing workstation where you draw sound directly on a CRT with a light pen.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Waveterm is the museum's cleanest example of direct-manipulation audio editing — a standalone computer whose primary instrument is a light pen used to draw and reshape single-cycle waveforms and wavetables directly on a green-phosphor CRT. It is a genuinely weird, interaction-model-rich artifact: composing and timbre-design happened by drawing, not by playing keys. Nothing in the museum's music/audio section uses light-pen waveform drawing (the existing music devices are keyboard, pad, keypad, or scanner-based). It is also a commercially odd and rare workstation (~300 units), paired as a companion computer to the PPG Wave synthesizer, and a canonical ancestor of sample editing and wavetable synthesis.",
