  "research": {
    "title": "PPG Waveterm B",
    "year": "1984",
    "by": "Palm Products GmbH (PPG), Hamburg — Wolfgang Palm",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Input", "Audio", "Light Pen", "Direct Manipulation"],
    "overview": "The PPG Waveterm is the standalone wave-computing companion to the PPG Wave synthesizer (1981–1987). Built on an Eltec Eurocom II computer with a Motorola 6809 CPU, the Waveterm A (1981–1983) and Waveterm B (1984–1987) were among the first commercial devices to offer direct-manipulation editing of sound via a graphic interface operated by a light pen. The user drew and reshaped single-cycle waveforms and wavetables on the CRT, ran Fourier analysis and resynthesis, additive synthesis, and stored results to diskette and later to the Wave's sample memory. It provided a sequencer and played a central role in the PPG Wave 2.3's 12-bit sampling. \n\nWolfgang Palm had pioneered wavetable synthesis with the Wavecomputer 360 in 1978, and the Waveterm was the tool that made those wavetables editable by hand. Instead of describing a sound numerically, the operator reached in with a light pen and sculpted the actual shape of the wave on screen. This is a distinct interaction paradigm — the machine answers to drawing, not to keyboard input — and a direct ancestor of modern sample editors and waveform DAWs. The Waveterm B screenshots preserved on Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0) show the actual light-pen drawing interface.\n\nApproximately 300 Waveterms were made. The Waveterm B could hold sampled single-cycle waveforms and was the only way to load samples into the Wave 2.3. It was used by notable electronic and pop artists of the mid-1980s and remains a sought-after rarity.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "Origins and the light-pen paradigm",
        "text": "Palm's wavetable synthesis stored up to 64 single-cycle waveforms per table; dynamically scanning through them produced moving timbres. The Waveterm made those waveforms authorable. Its defining interaction is the light pen: the operator points at the CRT to select, draw, and reshape a waveform's samples, and the machine renders the harmonic consequence. Fourier resynthesis let a user draw a partial spectrum and generate the corresponding wave. This is direct manipulation of sound before the word 'DAW' existed."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The hardware workstation",
        "text": "The Waveterm was a self-contained desktop computer (Eltec Eurocom II, Motorola 6809) with a green-phosphor CRT, a detached keyboard, an 8-inch floppy drive, and the custom PPG bus that connected it to the Wave synthesizer. The B revision added the ability to load 12-bit samples into the Wave 2.3, making it PPG's sampling front end. Editing functions — Compute a Wave, Compute a Wavetable, Create a Playcommand, Main Screen — were reached through the light pen and function keys, with the interface rendered in blocky graphic tiles on the CRT."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Significance for HCI",
        "text": "The Waveterm is a canonical early example of a graphic, drawing-driven editing interface applied to audio — a full decade before graphical audio editing became mainstream. It shows a producer operating on the visual shape of sound with a pen, in a commercial product, in the mid-1980s. It pairs naturally in the museum's collection with other light-pen/pointing devices (Gibson LPS, Vectrex Light Pen) and with the drawing-machine thread, but it is the only one applied to sound."
      }
    ],
